{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790517704,"nonce":"rhW4QsxoOIXVbM5XRGQiDzz7Py8=","title":"wasm AOT: a host collector cannot tell when runtime frames hold unscanned pointers","message":"A wasm host whose collector scans only linear memory (caribou's heap; Ash's hlp_runtime_callout_depth serves the same purpose on its side) must not collect at a poll inside compiled Wren while a WrenLift runtime frame below it is in a call into compiled code. Those Rust frames can keep object pointers in wasm locals, which no scan of linear memory finds.\n\nEvery wasm call from the runtime into compiled code goes through runtime_fns::call_entry: wren_call_N dispatch, closures, callbacks from natives. That is where the count belongs.\n\nwlift_aot_invoke_module_body and wlift_aot_run_programs also call compiled code, but their frames hold no heap pointers (a function pointer, a Vec of function pointers). Counting them would keep the depth at 1 or more for the whole of a program's run, so a host would never collect. They are left out.\n\nExport: wlift_runtime_callout_depth() -\u003e i32. It is per thread, raised around the call in call_entry, and always 0 off wasm. A host collects only while both its own count and this one are \u003c= 0.","files":null}]}